# OpenSTA uses C++20 three-way comparison (operator<=>) on std::string in
# network/Network.cc. The libc++ in older macOS SDKs (e.g. the Ventura
# Command Line Tools, MacOSX13.sdk) lacks operator<=> for std::basic_string,
# so the build fails. Replace those three sites with std::string::compare(),
# which has identical sign semantics and needs no newer standard library.
patchfiles-append   patch-Network-string-spaceship.diff

# write_path_spice gave a sequential cell's asynchronous set/reset pins no
# value, so they fell through to the tie low default in
# writeSubcktInstVoltSrcs() -- which for an active low set/reset is the
# ASSERTED level. The flop output is then held at a constant, never makes the
# transition the reported path took, and the deck's arrival measurements come
# up empty: every path launching from such a flop yields an unusable deck.
# Deassert those pins from the complement of the liberty clear/preset
# expressions instead. Submitted upstream as parallaxsw/OpenSTA issue #480;
# DROP this patch once the pinned commit above includes the fix (it will then
# fail to apply). Sibling of the #474/#475 non-unate side input fix, which is
# already in the pinned commit.
#
# Source only: the upstream change also adds test/write_path_spice_async_set_reset
# (a gzipped liberty among other files), which a unified diff cannot carry and
# this port does not run anyway.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-async-set-reset.diff

# Sibling defect to the one above, on the DATA side rather than the async
# set/reset side. write_path_spice chose a value for only ONE port of a
# sequential cell's next state expression: seqPortValues() called onePort(),
# which returns the first port it finds. For an enabled flop, next state is
# "(D & DE) | (IQ & !DE)" -- onePort() picks D, DE is never set, and DE falls
# through to the tie low default, so the enable is INACTIVE. The flop holds,
# the output never makes the transition the reported path took, and the deck's
# arrival measurements come up empty (7 of 15 paths on a sky130 picorv32,
# every one of them launching from an edfxtp).
#
# Now every settable input port of the next state gets the value that drives
# the output to the level the path needs. Cubes are taken under UNIVERSAL
# abstraction of the cell's internal state nodes (IQ/IQN): those cannot be
# driven in a deck, so a usable assignment has to hold for either value of
# them. That is what rejects the {DE=0} cubes -- they only reach the wanted
# level when the flop already happens to hold it -- and selects {D=1, DE=1}
# for a rise and {D=0, DE=1} for a fall.
#
# Not upstream: submitted, no response on the previous write_path_spice PR.
# Regression test, in the riscvpr repo (hd_ms has no enable flop, so only
# sky130 exercises this):
#   DIVERSE=1 PARA=route SKIP=1 zsh scripts/run_sky130_pico.sh
# then the in-flow deck audit must report seqbad 0.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-seq-enable.diff

# report_power aborted on any library whose power_lut_template names its slew
# axis input_net_transition rather than input_transition_time -- both are used
# in the wild, and charpy (icanalytica/simon) emits the former:
#
#   Critical 226: liberty/InternalPower.cc line 171, unsupported table axes
#
# InternalPowerModel::axisValue() accepted only input_transition_time, while
# the sibling GateTableModel::axisValue() has always accepted the pair, which
# is why timing reads the same library without complaint and only power dies.
# Nothing rejects the table at read time either: InternalPowerModel::checkAxes()
# is never called from LibertyReader (unlike the gate/check/receiver models),
# and its axis list was the timing-check set, disjoint from what axisValue()
# evaluates -- so the two could never agree. This accepts the alias and
# realigns checkAxis() with what axisValue() actually supports.
#
# NOT submitted upstream. Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it
# (it will then fail to apply).
patchfiles-append   patch-InternalPower-power-lut-axes.diff

# report_checks SIGSEGVs in the arnoldi delay calculator on a large design --
# ~976k cells with a 505 MB detailed (un-reduced) SPEF. read_liberty,
# read_verilog, link_design, read_sdc and read_spef all succeed; the crash is
# in the reducer, and the SAME design with the DEFAULT delay calculator reports
# fine, so it is arnoldi-specific rather than a size or memory limit (peak RSS
# 2.8 GB, deterministic across runs):
#
#   sta::ArnoldiReduce::setTerms(sta::ts_point *)
#   sta::ArnoldiReduce::makeRcmodelDrv()
#   sta::ArnoldiDelayCalc::findParasitic(...)
#   ... sta::Sta::findPathEnds(...)
#
# Three things in dcalc/ArnoldiReduce.cc combine. (1) The reset loop in
# loadWork() clears node_/eN/is_term but NOT tindex, and ts_pointV is a reused
# malloc'd pool, so internal nodes carry stale indices rather than zero.
# (2) findPt() cannot report failure: pt_map_ is read with operator[], which
# default-inserts 0 for an absent key, so a missing node silently yields
# &ts_pointV[0] -- normally an internal node, i.e. exactly the uninitialised
# case. (3) makeRcmodelDrv() checks neither the node nor the point before
# setTerms() uses tindex raw as an offset: `termV[0] = pdrv->tindex` then
# `p = pterm0 + termV[k]; outV[k] = p->ts` -- a wild read. It fires when a net's
# driver pin has no terminal point in that net's parasitic network, which is
# rare enough to need a very large design to hit with certainty.
#
# The patch initialises tindex to -1 so the condition is deterministic instead
# of depending on pool contents, and declines to reduce (returns nullptr, the
# function's existing "no reduced model" path, used three lines below for a
# degenerate ctot_) when the driver node is absent or is not a terminal.
#
# NOT submitted upstream, and NOT fixed there: ArnoldiReduce.cc is untouched
# since 2026-04-22, which predates the pinned commit, and no upstream issue or
# PR mentions arnoldi. Upstream did take the same shape of fix for the sibling
# calculator in ef0b6909 ("PrimaDelayCalc: handle degenerate parasitic
# networks", #476). Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it.
#
# UNPROVEN: applies cleanly and the guard is inert wherever tindex was valid,
# but the regression that matters -- that designs which already reduce produce
# byte-identical report_checks output -- has not been measured. If the guard
# starts declining reduction on nets that previously reduced, delays shift
# SILENTLY, which is worse than the crash.
patchfiles-append   patch-ArnoldiReduce-null-driver-term.diff

# LVF sigma on CHECK (setup/hold) arcs is silently dropped. When reading a
# constraint arc's LVF tables, LibertyReader asks for
#     ocv_sigma_{rise,fall}_constraiint      (and _std_dev_, _mean_shift_,
#                                             _skewness_ -- four names)
# -- "constraiint", double i. Liberty files spell it _constraint, so the lookup
# never matches, no warning is issued, and with sta_pocv_mode normal or
# skew_normal setup/hold slack comes out as if the check arcs carried no
# statistical data at all. The delay-arc call just above (ocv_sigma_*_transition
# etc.) is spelled correctly and is not touched. Reported in
# icanalytica/path_diversity#150.
#
# POCV here is the OpenSTA 3.x RUNTIME mode, not the 2.x compile-time SSTA build:
# DelayNormal / DelaySkewNormal / PocvMode are always compiled in, and it is
# switched on with `set sta_pocv_mode normal` (or skew_normal) plus
# sta_pocv_quantile. The old set_pocv_sigma_factor command no longer exists, and
# the commands live in the sta:: namespace, so `info commands *pocv*` at global
# scope finds nothing -- that is not evidence of a non-POCV build.
#
# NOT fixed upstream: the misspelling is still present at parallaxsw master.
# NOT submitted upstream. Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it.
patchfiles-append   patch-LibertyReader-lvf-constraint-spelling.diff

# Under POCV every slack reporter except report_checks returns the wrong value,
# so a violating design reports clean. Statistical slack is (mean, sigma) and
# its pessimistic single value is ALWAYS mean - q*sigma -- low slack is the bad
# direction for setup and hold alike. ReportPath::reportSlack does that
# (EarlyLate::early()); two other paths do not:
#
#   WRONG SIGN. search/Search.i converts slack with delayAsFloat(slack,
#   min_max, sta) at seven sites (worst_slack_cmd/_scene,
#   total_negative_slack_cmd/_scene, endpoint_slack, PathEnd::slack,
#   Path::slack). For -max, MinMax::max() read as an EarlyLate is LATE, so
#   DelayOpsNormal::asFloat returns mean + q*sigma, the optimistic tail. That
#   feeds report_worst_slack, report_wns, report_tns, worst_slack and
#   total_negative_slack. The comparisons that pick the worst endpoint already
#   use early, so the right endpoints were chosen and then reported wrongly.
#
#   SIGMA IGNORED. get_property ... slack (pin slack_max/_min/..., path_end and
#   path slack) went through Properties::delayPropertyValue, which uses the
#   one-argument delayAsFloat() = mean. A new slackPropertyValue() converts with
#   early; delayPropertyValue itself is left alone because arrival/required
#   properties need different tails.
#
# Measured on a one-violator fixture (riscvpr tooltest t6), POCV normal, q=3:
#                         unpatched      patched
#   report_checks         -0.436396     -0.436396   (unchanged, was right)
#   report_worst_slack    +0.836396     -0.436396
#   report_wns             0.000000     -0.436396
#   report_tns            +0.836396     -0.436396
#   get_property slack    +0.200000     -0.436396
# Scalar mode (no sigma) is unaffected: early == late == mean there.
#
# NOT fixed upstream (present at parallaxsw master 7a034b56); not submitted.
# Same Search.i also converts PathEnd/Path `required` with minMax (lines
# ~1174/1224) where ReportPath uses the opposite tail -- probably the same
# class of bug, NOT addressed or tested here.
patchfiles-append   patch-POCV-slack-pessimistic-tail.diff

# CCS output-current waveforms are silently misread when the library's
# output_current_template spells its slew axis input_transition_time rather
# than input_net_transition. LibertyReader::readOutputWaveforms decides which
# table axis is slew by testing axis1 == input_net_transition ONLY; anything
# else falls into the else branch, which takes axis2 as slew and axis1 as load
# cap -- a silent transpose, no warning. GateTableModel::axisValue has always
# accepted both spellings, so delay tables read fine and only the waveform
# path is affected, i.e. the ccs_ceff and prima delay calculators.
#
# Measured, one INV with an RC net from SPEF, ccs_ceff, rise delay at u1/Z:
#                      input_net_transition   input_transition_time
#   unpatched                0.039784               0.046464
#   patched                  0.039784               0.039784
# Unpatched, the misspelled-axis library quietly lands on (near) the NLDM
# answer instead of using its CCS data -- a 17% difference on this fixture.
# dmp_ceff_elmore (NLDM) is identical across all four, as it should be.
#
# The library-side spelling is the Liberty-correct one for CCS templates, so
# this is tool robustness, consistent with GateTableModel. The related
# OutputWaveforms::checkAxes is never called; NOTE if anyone wires it up that it
# dereferences axis2/axis3 without null checks and would crash on a 2-D
# template. Not fixed upstream (present at parallaxsw master); not submitted.
patchfiles-append   patch-LibertyReader-ccs-slew-axis-spelling.diff

# OpenSTA is C++20 and its headers rely on P0634 ("Down with typename!"), which
# lets `typename` be omitted in dependent contexts -- e.g. include/sta/Iterator.hh
# declaring VECTOR_TYPE::const_iterator without it. clang only implemented P0634
# in clang 16, which Apple ships as AppleClang 15. On a machine whose compiler is
# older the build dies with dozens of:
#   include/sta/Iterator.hh:64:3: error: missing 'typename' prior to dependent
#   type name 'VECTOR_TYPE::const_iterator'
#
# This is easy to misdiagnose because it does NOT track the OS version: two
# macOS 13.7 boxes differed only in that one had Xcode.app (AppleClang 15) while
# the other had just the Command Line Tools (AppleClang 14.0.3) -- same OS, same
# SDK, same pinned commit, and only the CLT-only machine failed. Note also that
# `/usr/bin/clang++ --version` in a login shell can report the CLT compiler while
# MacPorts' build environment resolves to Xcode's, so trust the
# "CXX compiler identification" line in the build log, not the shell.
#
# Blacklisting instead of hardcoding a compiler: MacPorts then keeps using the
# system compiler wherever it is new enough, and only falls back to a MacPorts
# clang (adding the matching build dependency itself) where it is not.
compiler.blacklist-append {clang < 1500}

# bison (>= 3.2), flex and swig (>= 3.0, with the Tcl backend) generate the
# parsers and Tcl bindings at build time. The macOS system bison/flex are too
# old, so the MacPorts copies are used explicitly below.
depends_build-append \
                    port:bison \
                    port:flex \
                    port:swig \
                    port:swig-tcl

# tcl       command interpreter (required)
# eigen3    linear algebra (required, header-only)
# libfmt10  provides the fmt library OpenSTA uses when the toolchain lacks
#           std::format. Without it OpenSTA's CMake clones fmt via FetchContent
#           (network mid-build) and then installs that fmt into the prefix,
#           squatting on the canonical fmt paths. See fmt_DIR below.
depends_lib-append  port:tcl \
                    port:eigen3 \
                    port:libfmt10

# OpenSTA's bundled cmake/FindTCL.cmake hard-codes Homebrew search paths on
# macOS and sets NO_DEFAULT_PATH, so it cannot locate the MacPorts Tcl (or
# tclreadline) unless pointed at ${prefix}/lib explicitly.
#
# Tcl readline support is forced OFF here so the build does not silently link a
# tclreadline that happens to be installed without declaring it; the +readline
# variant turns it on with the dependency declared.
# The MacPorts libfmt10 port installs its CMake package config into a versioned
# subdir (${prefix}/lib/libfmt10/cmake/fmt) that is off CMake's default search
# path, so find_package(fmt) misses it and OpenSTA falls back to FetchContent.
# Point fmt_DIR at it directly so the installed fmt is used and nothing is
# fetched or installed at build time. (Update the path if the libfmt
# dependency version changes.)
configure.args-append \
                    -DBISON_EXECUTABLE=${prefix}/bin/bison \
                    -DFLEX_EXECUTABLE=${prefix}/bin/flex \
                    -DTCL_LIB_PATHS=${prefix}/lib \
                    -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=OFF \
                    -Dfmt_DIR=${prefix}/lib/libfmt10/cmake/fmt

# CUDD improves conditional-arc, constant- and power-propagation handling and
# is enabled by default. CUDD installs cudd.h into ${prefix}/include and
# libcudd into ${prefix}/lib, which is exactly where OpenSTA's FindCUDD.cmake
# looks when handed -DCUDD_DIR=${prefix}.
variant cudd conflicts basic description {Build against the CUDD decision-diagram package} {
    depends_lib-append      port:cudd
    configure.args-append   -DCUDD_DIR=${prefix}
}

# Opt out of CUDD: `port install OpenSTA +basic`.
variant basic conflicts cudd description {Build without CUDD (fewer optimizations)} {}

default_variants    +cudd

# tclreadline (MacPorts port:tclreadline) adds command-line history and editing
# to the interactive `sta` shell. Irrelevant to batch/scripted runs, so it is
# off by default; enable with `port install OpenSTA +readline`. The base
# -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=OFF above is overridden here (cmake takes the last value).
variant readline description {Interactive command-line editing via tclreadline} {
    depends_lib-append      port:tclreadline
    configure.args-append   -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=ON
}
